# Auth Patterns (SaaS)

## Principles

- Authenticate on the **server**. Never trust client-only checks for entitlement.
- Prefer **session cookies** (httpOnly, secure, sameSite) for first-party web apps.
- Separate **identity** (who is logged in) from **authorization** (what they can do / paid plan).
- After login, load subscription status from your DB (synced via Stripe webhooks), not from JWT claims alone unless you refresh them.

## Next.js placement

- `middleware.ts`: redirect unauthenticated users from `(app)` routes; keep logic fast (JWT/session cookie check).
- Server Components / Server Actions: call `auth()` / `currentUser()` and `notFound()` or `redirect()` when missing.
- API Route Handlers: reject with `401` if no session; `403` if wrong role/plan.

## Roles & plans

- Roles: e.g. `user`, `admin`. Plans: e.g. `free`, `pro`. Model both; do not overload one field.
- Gate features with a single helper: `requireUser()`, `requirePlan("pro")`.
- Admin surfaces: separate route group + explicit role check.

## Provider notes

- **Auth.js (NextAuth)**: Credentials only if you need them; prefer OAuth/magic link. Use database sessions for easier revocation.
- **Clerk / similar**: Use their server helpers; still sync `userId` into your Prisma `User` on first login webhook/callback.
- On signup: create Prisma user, optional Stripe customer, default free plan — in one transaction when possible.

## Anti-patterns

- Hiding buttons as the only “auth”.
- Putting API keys or long-lived tokens in `localStorage` for first-party sessions.
- Checking plan only in middleware without re-checking on sensitive Server Actions.
